        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 902,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 1316,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Stretches or squeezes a tensor to a new size, filling in the values between the original ones. With one size, the first dim is resampled: a movement of 47 frames becomes 100, so takes of different lengths can be compared or averaged frame by frame;\na short curve drawn by hand becomes a smooth one. With two sizes, the first two dims are resized: an image (height x width x colours) at a new resolution.\n\nAny dims after the resized ones are carried along unchanged: 47 frames x 72 values resampled to 100 gives 100 x 72.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.interpolate <size> <size>\nDefault: 100.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor. Every input sends.\n\nsize (text box, also an inlet):\nThe new size: one number, or two (or three) for that many leading dims.\n\nmode:\nAn option, under the node. linear (the default) draws straight lines between the values; cubic draws smooth curves through them; nearest repeats the closest value (steps, and keeps labels as labels); area averages (best for shrinking).\n\nalign corners:\nAn option, under the node. On (the default), the first and last values stay exactly first and last; off, each value is treated as the centre of a cell, as images usually are.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresized:\nThe tensor at its new size.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nEight random values, new every second, resampled to 64. Linear (left) joins them with straight lines; cubic (right) passes smoothly through the same points.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.interp interpolates at positions you choose. scipy.cubic_spline gives a curve through points. scipy.resample_poly changes the sample rate of a signal without blurring its frequencies.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1300,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Stretches or squeezes a tensor to a new size, filling in the values between the original ones. With one size, the first dim is resampled: a movement of 47 frames becomes 100, so takes of different lengths can be compared or averaged frame by frame;\na short curve drawn by hand becomes a smooth one. With two sizes, the first two dims are resized: an image (height x width x colours) at a new resolution.\n\nAny dims after the resized ones are carried along unchanged: 47 frames x 72 values resampled to 100 gives 100 x 72.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.interpolate <size> <size>\nDefault: 100.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor. Every input sends.\n\nsize (text box, also an inlet):\nThe new size: one number, or two (or three) for that many leading dims.\n\nmode:\nAn option, under the node. linear (the default) draws straight lines between the values; cubic draws smooth curves through them; nearest repeats the closest value (steps, and keeps labels as labels); area averages (best for shrinking).\n\nalign corners:\nAn option, under the node. On (the default), the first and last values stay exactly first and last; off, each value is treated as the centre of a cell, as images usually are.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresized:\nThe tensor at its new size.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nEight random values, new every second, resampled to 64. Linear (left) joins them with straight lines; cubic (right) passes smoothly through the same points.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.interp interpolates at positions you choose. scipy.cubic_spline gives a curve through points. scipy.resample_poly changes the sample rate of a signal without blurring its frequencies."
        },
